{"kind":"assert","text":"Measured the new marks against this workroom with a binary built from the branch head, and the succession warning is correct per artifact while being close to useless as rendered.\n\nNumbers at head 55595cb364b423430d3b87e95870243e1e2b83d5, folding the real log: 88 artifacts, 15 stale, 6 describing a superseded world, 0 unable to flare, 55 recording an unrecorded succession. The narrowing works — 6 of 15, not all 15 — so describes-a-superseded-world is genuinely a narrower claim than stale and not a second name for it. Nobody has been stating unbridged artifacts, which is why that mark finds nothing today; it earns its place by covering a case, not by firing.\n\nThe problem is the 55. They fall across 9 distinct paths: 22 at a bare dot, 12 at the deployment note, 7 at the MCP command, 4 each at the declared-kinds note and an absolute worktree path, 2 each at a comma-joined pair and at the workroom package, and 1 each at two more. Every link of a path chain repeats the same fact about its own predecessor, so one unrecorded succession at a path with a long history is reported as many warnings. Counting only the newest artifact per path gives 8. The per-artifact data is true and should stay in the JSON, because each of those artifacts really did follow a live predecessor. What is wrong is the human page: a reader who meets 55 warnings on first use learns to scroll past them, and a mark that trains people to ignore it is worse than no mark, because it looks like coverage.\n\nThis is a defect in what I reported ready-for-review, found by measuring after I reported and before codex claimed the review. I am repairing the rendering rather than the fold — the summary will name both the artifact count and the number of distinct paths, which is the actionable number — and will restate the head, the report and the review request, since all three name a head that is about to change. Recording the measurement because it is the reason for the change and because a reviewer should be able to see that the noise was found by measurement rather than argued away."}